WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.422
The first way that we will
construct evolutionary trees is

00:00:03.422 --> 00:00:04.934
by using a distance matrix.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:09.427
And one of the most common ways of
constructing such a matrix is to begin

00:00:09.427 --> 00:00:11.267
with a multiple alignment.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:15.249
Here is a toy multiple alignment
that we'll use throughout this talk.

00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:20.415
We can form a distance matrix from this
multiple alignment in which D sub i,j

00:00:20.415 --> 00:00:24.843
is simply the number of differing
symbols between the i-th and

00:00:24.843 --> 00:00:27.555
j-th rows of the multiple alignment.

00:00:27.555 --> 00:00:32.228
i.e., there are three mismatches
between the first and second rows.

00:00:32.228 --> 00:00:39.107
So we simply set D sub i,2 and D sub 2,1
of this distance matrix equal to 3.

00:00:39.107 --> 00:00:43.509
Of course, we have encountered several
other different notions of distance

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:47.110
already that we could also use
to construct a distance matrix.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.639
But constructing a distance matrix from
a multiple alignment is very common.

00:00:51.639 --> 00:00:55.889
In order to construct a multiple
alignment of coronaviruses,

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:59.977
researchers chose the spike protein,
which identifies and

00:00:59.977 --> 00:01:03.837
binds to receptor sites on
the host's cell membrane.

00:01:03.837 --> 00:01:07.053
Here is the spike protein for
the SARS coronavirus.

00:01:07.053 --> 00:01:12.457
You may have noticed that evolutionary
trees take the shape of a graph.

00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:16.282
So we'll define a tree as a connected,
acyclic graph.

00:01:16.282 --> 00:01:21.529
Because the tree is connected, it means
that it holds the tree in one piece.

00:01:21.529 --> 00:01:25.993
And because it's acyclic,
it means that the tree can branch out

00:01:25.993 --> 00:01:29.805
without growing back in on itself and
forming a cycle.

00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:32.115
When we use a tree to model a phylogeny,

00:01:32.115 --> 00:01:35.968
the present day species should be
at the ending nodes of the tree.

00:01:35.968 --> 00:01:40.765
More formally, these ending nodes are
called "leaves", which I've shown in dark in

00:01:40.765 --> 00:01:44.546
this tree of life and
they're simply nodes that have degree one.

00:01:44.546 --> 00:01:49.051
The remaining nodes, shown in a lighter
color, are called "internal nodes", and

00:01:49.051 --> 00:01:52.189
they're simply nodes of
a larger degree than one.

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:56.734
Now, trees come in all different shapes and
sizes, but the fact that they

00:01:56.734 --> 00:02:01.459
are connected in a cyclic by definition
leads to some common properties.

00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:06.172
For example, we can always find two
leaves in a tree as long as the tree

00:02:06.172 --> 00:02:08.626
has at least two nodes, of course.

00:02:08.626 --> 00:02:13.015
Also, a tree with n nodes
has exactly n - 1 edges.

00:02:13.015 --> 00:02:15.778
For example,
the tree on the left has 14 nodes.

00:02:15.778 --> 00:02:20.009
So regardless of what shape the tree
has or how it's nodes are connected,

00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:22.136
we know that it must have 13 edges.

00:02:22.136 --> 00:02:26.862
In order to model the most common
ancestor of all the species in trees,

00:02:26.862 --> 00:02:29.597
we will sometimes place a "root" in a tree.

00:02:29.597 --> 00:02:34.179
If a tree is rooted, shown here by
the green node, then the root can be

00:02:34.179 --> 00:02:38.446
drawn at the top of tree with
the leaves at the bottom of the tree.

00:02:38.446 --> 00:02:43.204
By reorienting the tree in this way,
we know that time flows

00:02:43.204 --> 00:02:48.153
downward from the root to the leaves
in the sense that each edge

00:02:48.153 --> 00:02:53.213
of the tree connects an older
species to a more recent species.

00:02:53.213 --> 00:02:56.359
For now though, let's assume that
we are working with unrooted trees.

00:02:56.359 --> 00:02:59.037
This leads us to a computational

00:02:59.037 --> 00:03:03.804
problem of finding the tree
that "fits" a distance matrix.

00:03:03.804 --> 00:03:08.494
Of course, I should have known that
Son would somehow show up today.

00:03:08.494 --> 00:03:12.114
The problem that he has is
that we have been careless and

00:03:12.114 --> 00:03:16.608
we haven't defined what we mean for
a tree to "fit" a distance matrix.

00:03:16.608 --> 00:03:21.414
If we come back to the toy distance
matrix that we had for four species,

00:03:21.414 --> 00:03:24.927
then I will go ahead and
show you a tree that fits it.

00:03:24.927 --> 00:03:25.862
Here it is.

00:03:25.862 --> 00:03:29.764
We will need to assign weights
to the edges of this tree so

00:03:29.764 --> 00:03:33.916
that the sum of weights along
a path that connects two leaves

00:03:33.916 --> 00:03:38.420
corresponds to the distance matrix
value for those two leaves.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.426
Here we have weights of 1 and 2 and

00:03:40.426 --> 00:03:44.263
they combine to give us a distance
of 3 between chimp and human.

00:03:44.263 --> 00:03:47.786
The distance between chimp and
seal is 6, and

00:03:47.786 --> 00:03:51.875
edge weights 1, 3, and
2 give us 6, and so on.

00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:56.491
So you can verify that each
possible path through this tree,

00:03:56.491 --> 00:04:00.472
if you add up the weights of
the edges along the path,

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:03.747
that gives you the distance matrix value.

00:04:03.747 --> 00:04:09.448
Now we will use lower case d to indicate
the distance between leaves and a tree.

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:12.309
If you're given a tree
whose edges are weighted,

00:04:12.309 --> 00:04:16.601
then it's a pretty straightforward
problem to just compute the distances

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:18.998
between each pair of leaves in that tree.

00:04:18.998 --> 00:04:23.317
Of course, what we really want to
do is solve the reverse problem,

00:04:23.317 --> 00:04:28.272
which is finding a tree whose distance
between leaves fits a given matrix.

00:04:28.272 --> 00:04:30.971
You think this is now well defined?

00:04:30.971 --> 00:04:36.199
If you're not sure what I'm
driving at with this question,

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:41.028
feel free to pause and
take moment to try this exercise.

00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:52.290
Regardless of how long you spend
on this exercise that I gave you,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.889
you will never find a tree fitting it,
because such a tree simply does not exist.

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:01.614
For this reason, we need to distinguish
between distance matrices that have trees

00:05:01.614 --> 00:05:05.811
fitting them and distance matrices
that don't have trees fitting them.

00:05:05.811 --> 00:05:10.269
A matrix that does have a tree
fitting it is called "additive".

00:05:10.269 --> 00:05:14.862
There is another problem though,
which is that an additive matrix

00:05:14.862 --> 00:05:18.483
could have more than one
tree fitting it, and it will.

00:05:18.483 --> 00:05:23.776
Here's the tree we had before
fitting the toy distance matrix,

00:05:23.776 --> 00:05:28.578
but we can simply stretch out
the edges into longer paths and

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:32.614
still have a tree that
fits the distance matrix.

00:05:32.614 --> 00:05:35.719
But I would say that when
you look at these two trees,

00:05:35.719 --> 00:05:37.492
the top one just seems nicer,

00:05:37.492 --> 00:05:38.107
right?

00:05:38.107 --> 00:05:41.062
Of course, we need to rigorously
define what we mean by "nicer".

00:05:41.062 --> 00:05:45.914
The bottom tree has two
nodes of degree 2.

00:05:45.914 --> 00:05:49.753
So we can get the graph on the top
from the graph on the bottom by

00:05:49.753 --> 00:05:53.689
simply compressing all paths
that contain degree 2 nodes.

00:05:53.689 --> 00:05:58.655
This leads us to define a simple tree,
as a tree that has no nodes

00:05:58.655 --> 00:06:03.528
of degree 2, and the good news
is that if a matrix is additive,

00:06:03.528 --> 00:06:07.372
i.e., there is a tree fitting
this distance matrix,

00:06:07.372 --> 00:06:11.825
then there exists exactly one
simple tree that fits it.

00:06:11.825 --> 00:06:16.104
Now that we're armed with some
better terminology, we can return to

00:06:16.104 --> 00:06:21.271
the Distance-Based Phylogeny Problem that
we had before and restate it as finding

00:06:21.271 --> 00:06:26.550
the simple tree that fits a given distance
matrix, as long as that matrix is additive.

